From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:51:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] media: verisilicon: correct the JPEG encoder's maximum frame
 size

The Rockchip JPEG encoders advertise 8192x8192, one macroblock step more
than they can encode.  A dimension of exactly 8192 produces a JPEG whose
SOF marker reads 0x0 in that axis, with no error reported anywhere: the
encoder runs, the buffer is dequeued, and the picture is unusable.

The frame dimensions reach the hardware as macroblock counts through
nine-bit register fields -- VEPU_REG_MB_WIDTH() and VEPU_REG_MB_HEIGHT()
mask with 0x1ff, and the H1 pair occupy nine bits each -- so the largest
count that survives is 511, and 8192 is 512.  The mask makes it zero
rather than clamping it, which is why the header ends up describing a
frame of no size.

Advertise 8176 instead, which is 511 macroblocks and the last size that
encodes.

Observed on an RK3588 VEPU121 under both GStreamer v4l2jpegenc and FFmpeg.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/rockchip_vpu_hw.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/rockchip_vpu_hw.c b/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/rockchip_vpu_hw.c
index ef0f3fccc..f033a624c 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/rockchip_vpu_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/rockchip_vpu_hw.c
@@ -52,10 +52,10 @@ static const struct hantro_fmt rockchip_vpu_enc_fmts[] = {
 		.header_size = JPEG_HEADER_SIZE,
 		.frmsize = {
 			.min_width = 96,
-			.max_width = 8192,
+			.max_width = 8176,
 			.step_width = MB_DIM,
 			.min_height = 32,
-			.max_height = 8192,
+			.max_height = 8176,
 			.step_height = MB_DIM,
 		},
 	},
-- 
2.43.0
